Q: How to call a statically linked Pascal function from a C main? A: <p>The segfault happens because Pascal runtime isn't getting initialized before C calls into the Pascal code.</p>
<p>When you use <code>writeln</code>, Pascal expects its standard runtime structures to be alive and kicking. Calling it raw from <code>main()</code> skips that entire setup phase, so unit cleanup blows up on exit.</p>
<p>If you are open to switching from Free Pascal to GNU Pascal Compiler (GPC), it makes handling the runtime way simpler. You can explicitly initialize and tear down the Pascal environment right inside <code>main.c</code>.</p>
<ol>
<li>Tweak <code>hello.pas</code> to use GPC attribute syntax instead of the Delphi export style:</li>
</ol>
<pre><code>unit hello;

interface

procedure hello; attribute (name = 'hello');

implementation

procedure hello;
begin
    writeln('hello');
end;

end.
</code></pre>
<ol start="2">
<li>Wrap the function call in <code>main.c</code> with the GPC runtime hooks:</li>
</ol>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>void hello(void);

int main(int argc, char **argv, char **envp) {
    _p_initialize(argc, argv, envp, 0);
    hello();
    _p_finalize();
}
</code></pre>
<ol start="3">
<li>Compile and link both files straight with GPC:</li>
</ol>
<pre class="lang-bash prettyprint-override"><code>gpc -o hello hello.pas main.c
</code></pre>
<p>This lets GPC handle linking the runtime cleanly, and your static binary will run without crashing.</p>

Q: AlertDialog.Builder item background color A: <p>Try this:</p>

<pre><code>    final AlertDialog ad = new AlertDialog.Builder(new ContextThemeWrapper(this, R.style.AppTheme)).create();
</code></pre>

<p>add the buttons; and </p>

<pre><code>    for (int i = 0; i &lt; 2;i++) {
        Button b = ad.getButton(i);
        b.setBackgroundColor(color);
    }
    ad.show();
</code></pre>

<p>change your theme and color.</p>

